[{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Muvon/octobench/issues/2","repository_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Muvon/octobench","labels_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Muvon/octobench/issues/2/labels{/name}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Muvon/octobench/issues/2/comments","events_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Muvon/octobench/issues/2/events","html_url":"https://github.com/Muvon/octobench/issues/2","id":5288882404,"node_id":"I_kwDORQLyCs8AAAABOz3w5A","number":2,"title":"Proposal: EvalPort adapter for octobench's case.yaml / results.json (open interchange format for eval data)","user":{"login":"adhabnr-ux","id":237905827,"node_id":"U_kgDODi4now","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/237905827?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/adhabnr-ux","html_url":"https://github.com/adhabnr-ux","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/adhabnr-ux/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/adhabnr-ux/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/adhabnr-ux/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/adhabnr-ux/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/adhabnr-ux/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/adhabnr-ux/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/adhabnr-ux/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/adhabnr-ux/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/adhabnr-ux/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"labels":[],"state":"open","locked":false,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":0,"created_at":"2026-08-29T21:15:11Z","updated_at":"2026-08-29T21:15:11Z","closed_at":null,"assignee":null,"author_association":"NONE","issue_field_values":[],"type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"Hi — I read through `cli/main.py`, `scoring/aggregate.py`, `judges/llm_judge.py`, `runners/executor.py`, and a `case.yaml` under `cases/smoke/` before writing this, so this isn't a form pitch. I maintain [EvalPort](https://github.com/adhabnr-ux/evalport), an open, framework-agnostic interchange format (`TestCase` / `Grader` / `EvalSuite` / `ResultSet`, JSON Schema-backed) for portable LLM eval data — think \"a suite built for one eval tool can be read by another without hand-rewriting it.\" 30 framework adapters exist so far (DeepEval, Promptfoo, Inspect AI merged upstream, Opik, AutoGen, CrewAI, …), each a standalone `<tool>-openeval-adapter` package that maps the tool's native schema to EvalPort's, with zero changes to the tool itself.\n\noctobench's `case.yaml` + `results/<timestamp>/results.json` is a real, substantial data model — not the kind of thing I'd pitch this for if it were three fields. I think an `octobench-openeval-adapter` is a genuine fit, but there are also two real gaps I don't think EvalPort's current spec handles cleanly, and I'd rather surface those than oversell a clean 1:1 mapping.\n\n## What I found in the code\n\n**Case definition** (`cases/smoke/python/add_bug_fix/case.yaml`):\n```yaml\nid: smoke_python_add_bug_fix\nname: Python add() bug fix (docker smoke)\ncategory: dev\ndifficulty: simple\nsystem_prompt: |\n  You are a coding assistant working in a small Python project.\n  Make a minimal, targeted edit.\ninstruction: |\n  add.py has a bug: the function add(a, b) returns the wrong result.\n  Fix add.py so that all assertions in test_add.py pass.\n```\nplus `setup.sh` / `quality.sh` / `validate.sh` sibling scripts (`timeout_minutes` also read from the yaml in `cli/main.py`).\n\n**Per-run result record** (built in `cli/main.py`, one per case×provider×model, collected into `results/<timestamp>/results.json`):\n```python\nrecord = {\n    \"case_id\": case_id, \"provider\": provider_name, \"model\": benchmark_model,\n    \"result\": {\"stdout\": ..., \"stderr\": ..., \"exit_code\": ..., \"elapsed_ms\": ...},\n    \"tokens\": {  # scoring/aggregate.py's TOKEN_SEMANTICS = \"separate_reasoning_v1\"\n        \"semantics\": \"separate_reasoning_v1\", \"input\": ..., \"cached_input\": ...,\n        \"output\": ..., \"reasoning\": ..., \"total\": ...,\n    },\n    \"cost_usd\": eval_cost,\n    \"scripts\": {\"setup\": {...}, \"quality\": {...}, \"validate\": {...}},  # each {exit_code, stdout, stderr, elapsed_ms}\n    \"judge\": judge_out,   # from judges/llm_judge.py run_judge()\n    \"scoring\": {},        # filled by _score_record()\n}\n```\n`judges/llm_judge.py::run_judge` runs an LLM panel (`JUDGE_PANEL_ATTEMPTS = 3` retries per model) and returns `{score, reasoning, issues, confidence, judges: [{model, score, confidence, reasoning, issues, parse_error, elapsed_ms}, ...]}` — each candidate's `_is_valid_verdict` check is explicit about *why* (an empty-zero verdict is a failed parse, not a genuine 0).\n\n`scoring/aggregate.py::_score_record` then blends that with `compute_efficiency_score` (latency/tokens/cost, exponential decay against configurable baselines) into `scoring.final_score`, and applies a flat `validation_fail_penalty` (default 25.0) whenever `scripts.validate.exit_code != 0`.\n\n## Proposed mapping (`octobench-openeval-adapter`, same shape as `adapters/opik-openeval-adapter`)\n\n```python\ndef case_to_testcase(case: dict) -> dict:\n    return {\n        \"id\": case[\"id\"],\n        \"input\": case[\"instruction\"],\n        \"graders\": [\"gr_validate_exit\", \"gr_llm_judge_panel\"],\n        \"metadata\": {\n            \"category\": case.get(\"category\"),\n            \"difficulty\": case.get(\"difficulty\"),\n            \"com.octobench.system_prompt\": case.get(\"system_prompt\"),\n            \"com.octobench.setup_script\": \"setup.sh\",\n            \"com.octobench.validate_script\": \"validate.sh\",\n        },\n        \"timeout_ms\": int(case.get(\"timeout_minutes\", 30)) * 60_000,\n    }\n\nGRADERS = [\n    {\"id\": \"gr_validate_exit\", \"type\": \"code\",\n     \"params\": {\"language\": \"bash\", \"source\": \"validate.sh\"},\n     \"description\": \"fail-to-pass acceptance test; scripts.validate.exit_code\"},\n    {\"id\": \"gr_llm_judge_panel\", \"type\": \"llm_judge\",\n     \"params\": {\"model\": \"panel\", \"prompt\": \"judges/prompts.py::JUDGE_TEMPLATE\"}},\n]\n\ndef record_to_result(r: dict) -> dict:\n    validate_exit = r[\"scripts\"][\"validate\"][\"exit_code\"]\n    judge = r[\"judge\"]\n    panel_01 = (judge.get(\"score\") or 0) / 100.0   # octobench judges score 0-100\n    return {\n        \"test_case_id\": r[\"case_id\"],\n        \"actual_output\": r[\"result\"][\"stdout\"],\n        \"grader_results\": [\n            {\"grader_id\": \"gr_validate_exit\", \"type\": \"code\",\n             \"score\": 1.0 if validate_exit == 0 else 0.0, \"passed\": validate_exit == 0,\n             \"reason\": f\"validate.sh exit_code={validate_exit}\"},\n            {\"grader_id\": \"gr_llm_judge_panel\", \"type\": \"llm_judge\",\n             \"score\": round(panel_01, 4), \"passed\": panel_01 >= 0.7,  # octobench sets no pass bar here — see gap #2\n             \"reason\": judge.get(\"reasoning\"),\n             \"metadata\": {\"confidence\": judge.get(\"confidence\"), \"panel\": judge.get(\"judges\"),\n                          \"openeval.raw_score\": judge.get(\"score\")}},\n        ],\n        \"passed\": validate_exit == 0,\n        \"duration_ms\": r[\"result\"][\"elapsed_ms\"],\n        \"metadata\": {\n            \"openeval.cost\": r.get(\"cost_usd\"),\n            \"com.octobench.tokens\": r[\"tokens\"],\n            \"com.octobench.final_score\": r.get(\"scoring\", {}).get(\"final_score\"),\n            \"provider\": r[\"provider\"], \"model\": r[\"model\"],\n        },\n    }\n```\n\n## Two things I don't think map cleanly, stated honestly rather than glossed over\n\n1. **`TestCase.input` assumes a stateless prompt; octobench cases aren't that.** A case is really `(pinned repo checkout via setup.sh, mutation, instruction)` — the thing under test is a live workspace an agent edits over a multi-turn CLI session, and correctness is `validate.sh`'s exit code against that workspace, not a string comparison against `expected_output` (which octobench cases don't have at all). The mapping above smuggles the setup/validate scripts into `metadata.com.octobench.*`, which round-trips the *data* but isn't something a generic EvalPort consumer could execute without octobench-specific tooling — closer to how SWE-bench-style harnesses would hit the same wall. This might be a real case for a `profile:agentic_repo` convention (repo ref, script names, workdir diffing) rather than something I should paper over with metadata alone.\n\n2. **The composite `scoring.final_score` (judge×0.85 + efficiency×0.15, minus a validation penalty) doesn't have a natural `GraderResult` home.** It's not a correctness judgment — it's octobench's own ranking metric across latency/cost/tokens/correctness, and it's actually what BENCHMARK.md's leaderboard sorts by. I put it under `Result.metadata` above rather than inventing a third pseudo-grader for it, since scoring it as a \"grader\" would conflate \"did the agent get it right\" with \"was it cheap and fast,\" which the spec's `passed` semantics (Rule 6 / the `all` aggregation default) aren't meant to conflate either. Open question, not a solved one.\n\n## What I haven't done\n\nNo code exists yet — this issue is the proposal, not a PR. I haven't run this against a real `results.json`, so the sketch above is unverified against your actual field values (e.g. I'm inferring `judge.score` is 0-100 from `scoring/aggregate.py`'s `compute_final_score` clamping to `[0, 100]`, not from a captured run). If this is a direction you'd want, I'd build it as a standalone `octobench-openeval-adapter` package (import from `results.json` → `ResultSet`, `cases/**/case.yaml` → `EvalSuite`) with no changes to octobench itself, and open a PR against `adapters/` in the EvalPort repo — happy to also open a PR here only if you'd want octobench to depend on it directly, which I wouldn't assume by default.\n\nLet me know if the two gaps above are actually gaps, or if I'm missing existing octobench conventions (e.g. in `docs/EXTENDING.md`) that already resolve them.\n\n— Sahi, independent contributor (not affiliated with this project)","closed_by":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Muvon/octobench/issues/2/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Muvon/octobench/issues/2/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"pinned_comment":null}]